      {
        "kind": "review",
        "ok": false,
        "reason": "src/router/trie-router/node.ts search(): a request whose part literally equals a suffix-wildcard label (e.g. GET '/assets*' with route '/assets*') is matched both by the exact `node.#children[part]` lookup and by the new #prefixes loop, so the handler is returned twice (['a','a']) and middleware would run twice. Child nodes reached that way also get pushed twice. Skip the exact-child path for prefix labels, or skip the prefix loop when `part === label`.",
        "cost_usd": 0.2502604,
        "probed": true,
        "denied": []
      },
      {
        "kind": "verify",
        "ok": true,
        "reason": null,
        "cost_usd": null,
        "probed": null,
        "denied": null
      },
      {
        "kind": "review",
        "ok": true,
        "reason": "The fix in node.ts treats a trailing `*` in a segment as a literal-prefix match, and it does this in both the TrieRouter and the default router. I checked it through the full Hono app with the mounted sub-app from the task. `/assets*` matches `/assets`, `/assets/app.js` and deeper paths. `/file.+*` matches `/file.+js` but not `/fileZZjs`. It still matches when `/assets*/x` is registered first, and params and middleware still work. The existing router and hono tests pass (771 passed, 22 skipped).",
